📅 Day 14 – Polymorphism (Compile-Time & Runtime)

Full Stack Web Development with Java Internship

Today’s session focused on the concept of Polymorphism in Java, one of the core principles of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP). The mentor explained both compile-time polymorphism and runtime polymorphism with practical examples.

🔹 Topics Covered
1️⃣ Compile-Time Polymorphism (Method Overloading)

Compile-time polymorphism is achieved through method overloading.

Key points discussed:

Multiple methods share the same name

Methods differ in number, type, or order of parameters

Decision is made at compile time

Also called Static Binding

Understood how overloading improves flexibility and readability in programs.

2️⃣ Runtime Polymorphism (Method Overriding)

Runtime polymorphism is achieved through method overriding.

Key points discussed:

A subclass provides its own implementation of a method defined in the parent class

Requires inheritance (extends)

Method signature must be the same

Decision is made at runtime

Also called Dynamic Binding or Dynamic Method Dispatch

The mentor explained how the JVM decides which method to execute based on the object reference during runtime.

🔹 Dynamic Method Dispatch

Parent class reference can refer to child class object

The overridden method in child class is executed

Demonstrates runtime decision-making

This concept plays a major role in achieving flexibility and extensibility in Java applications.

🔹 Practical Implementation

Practiced writing programs for method overloading.

Implemented method overriding using parent and child classes.

Observed how runtime polymorphism works during execution.
